issues.body:
The draft has no Acknowledgments section. `grep -in acknowledg` on the source returns nothing, and the back matter ends at Document History.

It is optional for a -00 and idnits does not flag its absence, but this document has been through several rounds of substantive external review that changed the architecture, not just the wording. Examples of what came from review rather than the original text:

- the two-question framing that now opens the Introduction;
- separating identification from attribution, which removed the unconditional sender-constraint requirement;
- the Source Instance Identity and Instance Context Identifier vocabulary;
- replacing the fixed preservation hop limit with the authenticated-provenance rule;
- defining continuity by evidence rather than by key custody alone.

Crediting those reviewers by name is customary and costs a short back-matter section. Only the author knows who they are.

Place it after Document History, unnumbered, consistent with the other back-matter sections.
